---
import { MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_CARDINALIDADE } from "@ovh/otel-domain";
import { CardinalidadeLab } from "../../components/CardinalidadeLab.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/CardinalidadeLab.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="Cardinality and the bill — OpenLabs"
  description="A metric stream is identified by its attribute set, so the number of streams is the product of the distinct values — not the sum. One attribute with 8 000 values does not add 8 000 streams to a metric that had 288; it multiplies them."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>One attribute is not one more column</h1>
        <p>
          An account manager asks which customer is affected. You have a latency
          histogram with route, status and method on it, so you add
          <code>enduser.id</code> and ship it. The metric had 288 streams. It
          now has <b>2 304 000</b> — and nothing warned you, because nothing was
          wrong: a stream is identified by its attribute set, so attributes
          <b>multiply</b>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below is that arithmetic, with your numbers. Turn attributes on and
          off, and correct the value counts to your own service — the default
          numbers are a mid-sized shop and the only ones that matter are yours.
          Watch the product written out, not just the total: it is the whole
          argument.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>No simulation here, and that is deliberate.</p>
        <p>Cardinality is not a flow — it is a product. See <code>DECISIONS.md</code> §9.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <CardinalidadeLab client:visible />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Turn on <code>enduser.id</code>.</b> Four orders of magnitude, from
            one checkbox. This is the whole lab, and it is why the answer to
            &ldquo;can we break this down by customer?&rdquo; is never yes or no —
            it is a multiplication you do before answering.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Switch the instrument from Histogram to Counter.</b> The stream
            count does not move and the volume collapses, because a histogram
            publishes a point per bucket plus the sum and the count. A histogram
            is the expensive instrument <em>before</em> any attribute is added,
            and most teams reach for it by default.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Now turn on <code>cardinality_limit</code>.</b> The bill stops
            growing and the warning appears: the excess attribute sets fold into
            one <code>otel.metric.overflow</code> stream. Read that number
            carefully — it is how many breakdowns you are paying to produce and
            cannot read. The limit protects the invoice, not the question.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Set <code>k8s.pod.name</code> to 40 and think about deploys.</b>
            The product is over values actually <em>seen</em>, so each rollout
            retires 40 pod names and mints 40 more. The old streams stay until
            retention rolls them off — which is why cardinality incidents outlive
            the change that caused them by days.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>
          Answer first, then compute it above. Predicting and being wrong is what
          makes the answer stick.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="A histogram has http.route (12 values), http.response.status_code (6) and http.request.method (4). You add enduser.id, which has 8 000 values in production. How many streams does the metric have afterwards?"
          opcoes={[
            "2 304 000 — the attribute multiplies the existing streams",
            "8 288 — the existing 288 streams plus one per customer",
            "8 000 — one stream per customer, since that is the finest breakdown",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="12 × 6 × 4 = 288 streams before. A stream is identified by its full attribute set, so every one of those 288 now exists once per customer: 288 × 8 000 = 2 304 000. The sum reading (8 288) is the intuition that attributes are columns, and it is wrong by three orders of magnitude here — which is exactly how a cardinality incident starts, since the change looks like one line of code."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Every row below is a belief worth naming, because the arithmetic above
          is arranged to take it apart. The right column is the specification,
          with the link.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the spec says</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_CARDINALIDADE.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                  <td>
                    {m.spec}{" "}
                    <a href={m.fonte} rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">
                      source
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Check it against a real SDK</h2>
        <p>
          This lab is arithmetic, so it owes you a way to confirm the arithmetic
          is the SDK's and not ours. It is — and the counterpart already exists:
          <code>labs/three-pillars/</code> runs a real SDK against a real
          Collector, and <code>PILLARS_USER_ATTRIBUTE</code> is exactly the move
          this lab charges you for.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            In <code>labs/three-pillars/compose.yaml</code>, leave
            <code>PILLARS_USER_ATTRIBUTE</code> as <code>false</code> and run
            <code>docker compose up --build</code>. In the Collector's output,
            count the distinct attribute sets on
            <code>http.server.request.duration</code> — route times status.
          </li>
          <li>
            Set it to <code>true</code> and run again. The count is multiplied by
            the number of distinct users the app emits, not increased by it.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          That is the one claim this lab makes about the world, and it is
          checkable. <b>What it deliberately does not claim is a price:</b> every
          vendor bills on a different unit, so the lab computes volume — which is
          mechanical — and leaves the price field for you to fill from your own
          invoice. A number we invented would be the most expensive kind of
          mistake this project can make.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Read the theory</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel/articles/what-an-attribute-costs")}
            >What an attribute costs</a
          > is the prose version of this arithmetic, and it carries the part a
          calculator cannot: why the decision is taken in instrumentation code by
          one developer and paid for by everyone a month later. It starts from
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel/articles/what-a-signal-is")}>What a signal
          is</a> — a metric throws the individual away, and an attribute is how
          you take part of that back.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th,
  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
